# Workflow

```text
Grill (when the design branches) → Issue → branch → work → PR → human review → squash merge → retrospective
```

1. Grill when the API, the meaning, or a roadmap boundary branches. Skill: `.agents/skills/grilling/SKILL.md`. Skip it when the Issue already has acceptance text and `docs/design.ja.md` settles the question.
2. One Issue is one PR. Create the Issue from a template in `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/`. Blank issues are off. An implementation Issue's Steps take every item of its roadmap row's text, the items that earlier reviews handed to the row included: the T13 row asked for a variant that splits T12's shaping gap, Issue #437 left it out, and the row was set to `done` without it until the review found it (PR #438).
3. A change to the meaning of the design is a `design` Issue that updates `docs/design.ja.md` before any implementation Issue (`.cursor/rules/design-docs.mdc`).
4. Acceptance text lives on the Issue. `docs/roadmap.md` keeps ID, title, Issue, and status only. A spike's result goes into the `## Result` section of the Issue body (`gh issue edit --body-file`), with the evidence table and the reproduction commands, before the PR is called ready. A comment on the Issue does not fill it. The PR also sets its roadmap row to `done` and points **今** at the next row before it is called ready, a PR stacked on another one included (the S8b PR left them at `in-progress`; the L3 PR set its row to `done` and left **今** on L3; the T12 PR did the same, with the branch and the Issue of D9's design PR under it, PR #426's review). When **今** points at a row that is not started, its branch and Issue lines read "—": P02b's last PR pointed **今** at P02b2 and left the branch of P02b's first PR (PR #492's review). The next row is chosen by the classes of `.cursor/rules/pace.mdc` ("What comes next is decided, not read off a list"): a missing capability the requirements name comes before a figure to explain. Within a class, it is the first in the table that is not started and whose text puts it before the rows that remain, read in each row's text ("before V3", "before T1"), not only the next row down. After D3 the agent took the table's order alone and pointed **今** at Q2, Q3, and Q6, quality rows, while v0.2 (P02) and the rows before it were not started; the owner asked whether the main work had ended (PR #452). V3c's PR pointed **今** at M1b, the row after V3, while I1's text said it comes before V3 and D9's that it is decided before T1; both were above V3 and not started, and the owner chose I1 (PR #381's retrospective). When the owner picks the next row out of the table's order, the PR that starts that row (its design PR) also moves the row to its place in the table: V2's design PR pointed **今** at V2 and left its row after R5, D9, and T1, and the owner asked (PR #344). An acceptance figure says what it measures and how, and is one that the compared frameworks can meet: T4's "position error under ⅛ px" was the bound of the rounding, not what is measured from pixels, where all three frameworks exceed ⅛ (PR #173). When a row is split, each reference to the old ID in `docs/` is pointed at the row that now holds the item it names; a reference that means the whole group ("T12 は P02 の前") keeps the old ID. D5 pointed 47 references at P02a–P02g, DD1, and P03a/b and kept 8 group references, with no rule to say which (PR #453).
5. Branch from `main`: `type/<issue-number>-<slug>`, for example `feat/12-arena`. `type` is a Conventional Commits type.
6. Open the PR in the same turn that the branch is pushed. Use `.github/pull_request_template.md` (`.cursor/rules/pull-requests.mdc`). `Closes #N` stands alone on a line under `## Related` (`.cursor/rules/github-closing.mdc`). Before calling a PR ready, and before answering a review, read the checks of the pushed head (`gh pr checks <n>`): a local `./build.ps1` does not run on the runners. In L1b the CI of 9d71679 failed while the report said the gate passed. A PR that shows no checks is first checked for a conflict with `main` (`gh pr view <n> --json mergeable`): the pull_request workflow does not run on a conflicting PR, and a run started by hand (`gh workflow run`) does not show as the PR's checks (PR #102). After a job is run again, its result is read by the run's attempt (`gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/runs/<id>` gives `run_attempt`, and `.../attempts/<n>/jobs` the jobs of one): read right after asking for the rerun, the job still shows the attempt before. On PR #313 a watcher took the second attempt's success for the third and asked for one rerun too many. After a new head is pushed, a job is run again only on that head: `ci.yml` cancels the runs of a branch in progress (`concurrency`, `cancel-in-progress`), so a rerun of the old head's run cancels the new head's. On PR #432 a rerun of `526c3f6`'s Windows job cancelled `6c2e8bf`'s Windows and macOS jobs; the macOS job showed cancelled after its gate had passed, and was first taken for a fault of the runner.
7. A human reviews and squash-merges. An agent merges only when the owner names the PR and orders it (L1a: "コンフリクトを修正してマージしてください" for #85); it squash-merges after the checks pass, with one message in the form of `.cursor/rules/conventional-commits.mdc`.
8. After the merge, delete the local and remote branch. After stacked PRs are squash-merged, `git merge-base --is-ancestor` cannot show that a commit reached `main` (the squash made a new commit); search `main` for what the last commit changed instead.
9. Retrospective, below, before the next feature branch.

## Issue templates and labels

Labels have the form `<axis>:<value>`. The only axis today is `type`.

| Template   | Label           | Commit / PR type           |
| ---------- | --------------- | -------------------------- |
| `feat`     | `type:feat`     | `feat`                     |
| `spike`    | `type:spike`    | `chore` (code is scratch)  |
| `bug`      | `type:bug`      | `fix`                      |
| `refactor` | `type:refactor` | `refactor`                 |
| `test`     | `type:test`     | `test`                     |
| `design`   | `type:docs`     | `docs`                     |
| `task`     | `type:chore`    | `chore` / `build` / `ci`   |

## Gates

- `./build.ps1`: tool restore, CSharpier check, restore, then Release and Debug builds with warnings as errors, each followed by every `tests/**/*.Tests.csproj`.
- The NativeAOT publish check runs where the Issue names it (`.cursor/rules/aot.mdc`).
- Benchmarks are not a gate. The Zero-Alloc tests are (`.cursor/rules/bench.mdc`).
- After each merge, read `main`'s CI. A red `main` gets its own Issue and PR before the next merge.

## Forbidden

- Starting implementation without an Issue number and a branch.
- Treating a roadmap title, or a file under `.dev/`, as permission to code.
- Copying acceptance text onto the roadmap.
- Taking a row out of the roadmap's order without moving it in `docs/roadmap.md` in the same design PR. K1 was chosen before rows listed above it, and the owner had to ask for the roadmap to follow (PR #352's Grill).
- Adding or removing roadmap rows, or touching **Intentionally out of scope**, without a Grill. A row that a measurement shows to be needed is put to the owner as a question with a recommendation before the PR: R4c added row Q4 for the layers' GPU time inside its PR, with no Grill (PR #264's retrospective).
- Merging. Closing an Issue or PR without an order that names it.
- Ending a turn on a branch that has no Issue and no PR.

## Numbers

Create an Issue before its number is written anywhere: a branch name, a commit, a PR body, an ADR. Issue and PR numbers share one sequence; the next one is not predictable.

```text
# BAD — write `Closes #8` into the body, then create the Issue, which gets #9
# GOOD — create the Issue, read its number from the output, then write the body
```

## Stacked PRs

A second Issue that builds on an open PR gets its own branch and PR based on the first. A stacked PR is merged only after its base has merged. When the base merges, retarget the next PR to `main` first, then delete the merged branch. Before `gh pr create` on a stacked base, check that the base branch still exists (`git ls-remote --heads origin <base>`): I1c's PR was about to stack on #408 when the owner merged #406 and #408, GitHub deleted the base, and the create failed. Opened on `main`, a branch that had merged the base's own commits showed them again in its diff, since `main` holds them squashed; merge `main` into the branch before the PR, so that the diff is the branch's own (PR #410).

## Retrospective

After a squash merge, in the turn that learns of it, write three points as a comment on the merged PR. Write "none" when a point is empty.

1. A lesson for `.cursor/rules/`, a skill, `docs/design.ja.md`, or one ADR under `docs/adr/`. Tacit knowledge the work relied on is a lesson: a way of measuring, a way a tool fails on this setup, a mistake the review or the owner had to catch.
2. The Issue that will carry it, if any.
3. Whether to refactor. Yes opens a `refactor` Issue that comes next. Postponing it needs a roadmap row (`.cursor/rules/defer.mdc`).

Then, without waiting for an ask, file a `chore` Issue and one PR that writes each lesson into the file it names. The next feature branch waits until that PR is open.

```text
# BAD — the PR merged; the next row starts; what the row taught stays in the conversation
# GOOD — the PR merged; three points on it; a chore PR with the lessons is open; then the next row
```
